description:
When creating sub-dissectors, the `dissect_thrift_t_<type>()` functions did not update the `thrift_opt->previous_field_id` value.

While the sub-dissectors could do it themselves, it was tedious and error-prone. Additionally, if it was not done, the generic dissector could erroneously detect a field order mismatch, generating a warning in the tree.

```c
// None of the previous calls modified thrift_opt->previous_field_id, it’s still 0
offset = dissect_thrift_t_string(tvb, pinfo, omega_tree, offset, thrift_opt, TRUE, 6, hf_omega_create_user_user_id);
// 6 is greater than 0, no problem. But no update either, so thrift_opt->previous_field_id = 0
offset = dissect_thrift_t_struct(tvb, pinfo, omega_tree, offset, thrift_opt, TRUE, 5, hf_omega_create_user_user_fields, ett_omega_user_db_record, omega_user_db_record);
// Issue 1: 5 is greater than 0, no problem detected although we should have seen that 5 is less than 6.
// Then, the structure omega_user_db_record contains 17 fields numbered 0 to 16, so now thrift_opt->previous_field_id = 16
offset = dissect_thrift_t_bool(tvb, pinfo, omega_tree, offset, thrift_opt, TRUE, 7, hf_omega_create_user_enable_progress);
// Issue 2: This call "saw" that 7 is less than 16, raising the issue thrift.unordered_field_id
```

The purpose of this patch is to do the update automatically instead of relying on the sub-dissector to update `previous_field_id` after each call.

There are 2 additional trivial fixes in the patch:
* `TBP_THRIFT_FID_LEN` was used instead of `TBP_THRIFT_I16_LEN` (both with the same value 2 so no impact on the behavior).
* The message itself was a copy of the one from thrift.undefined_field_id.

